 # Method-Specific Training Configurations
-
-
 ## Overview
-Method-Specific Training Configurations define the hyperparameters, optimization loops, and architectural adjustments required to train specific Neural Radiance Field (NeRF) variants. This family of methods solves the problem of translating theoretical NeRF formulations into stable, convergent training procedures for diverse datasets and hardware constraints. It sets itself apart from sibling methods like [Training Loop Setup](1522-training-loop-setup.md) and [Learning Rate Scheduling](2701-learning-rate-scheduling.md) by providing the concrete numerical values, loss functions, and network structures unique to each variant (e.g., NeRF, Mip-NeRF, DONeRF), rather than generic procedural steps.
+Method-Specific Training Configurations specify the hyperparameters, optimization loops, and architectural adjustments required to train specific Neural Radiance Field (NeRF) variants [S18780-multi-scale-and-mip-aware-representations.md]. This family provides the concrete numerical values, loss functions, and network structures unique to each variant, distinguishing it from generic procedural steps like [Training Loop Setup](1522-training-loop-setup.md) or [Learning Rate Scheduling](2701-learning-rate-scheduling.md).
 
 ## Shared principles
-The children in this family share the fundamental goal of minimizing the photometric error between rendered images and ground truth inputs via gradient descent. All methods utilize differentiable volume rendering to compute gradients with respect to the neural network parameters. Common structural elements include the use of Multi-Layer Perceptrons (MLPs) to map spatial coordinates and viewing directions to density and color, and the use of ray sampling strategies to query these networks. However, the specific activation functions, sampling densities, loss formulations, and optimizer settings vary significantly to address artifacts like aliasing, dark pixels, or memory constraints.
+The methods in this family share the fundamental goal of minimizing the photometric error between rendered images and ground truth inputs via gradient descent [S18780-multi-scale-and-mip-aware-representations.md]. All methods utilize differentiable volume rendering to compute gradients with respect to the neural network parameters [S18780-multi-scale-and-mip-aware-representations.md]. Common structural elements include the use of Multi-Layer Perceptrons (MLPs) to map spatial coordinates and viewing directions to density and color, and the use of ray sampling strategies to query these networks [S18780-multi-scale-and-mip-aware-representations.md]. However, the specific activation functions, sampling densities, loss formulations, and optimizer settings vary significantly to address artifacts like aliasing, dark pixels, or memory constraints [S18780-multi-scale-and-mip-aware-representations.md].
 
