+### CoroNeRF Training Configuration
+A configuration for joint thermodynamic reconstruction and error localization in neural tomography [S10, unofficial]. It uses a multiresolution hash grid with a $2^{20}$ hashmap, $\times 2$ features, and 20 levels (16 to 512) [S10, unofficial]. The decoder has 2 hidden layers of width 64 [S10, unofficial]. It uses the AdamW optimizer with learning rate $5 \times 10^{-4}$ and weight decay $10^{-5}$ [S10, unofficial]. Joint reconstructions optimize using batches of 1024 rays for 60k steps, while density-only representations use 30k steps [S10, unofficial]. The loss is an asinh loss with scales $s_c = [1.3 \times 10^{-2}, 4.5 \times 10^{-4}, 10^{-2}, 5.8 \times 10^{-2}]$ for Fe XIII 1075, Fe XIII 1080, Si IX 2585, Si IX 3935 respectively [S10, unofficial]. GPU hashgrid optimization is nondeterministic, so ensemble seeds (e.g., 0xC0FFEE, 0xDECAF) are used for reproducibility [S10, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The original NeRF paper [S1] established the baseline training configuration, which has been adapted for various datasets (Blender, LLFF) and variants (Mip-NeRF, DONeRF, etc.). Subsequent works introduced specific optimizations to address issues like aliasing (Mip-NeRF), computational cost (DONeRF), defocus (AR-NeRF), and memory constraints (DP-based Selector). The field has evolved from simple MLP-based NeRFs to more complex architectures involving hash encodings (Instant-NGP, Neuralangelo) and GAN-based frameworks (StyleNeRF, AR-NeRF), each requiring tailored training configurations.
